A||||7||Seanh||3.4 Performance Headers Question||||6||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-05-2000||10:39 PM||||What headers should I go with on my 3.4 swap? I am not usally a street racer, so I dont need high hp ones. I want the engine geared tword torque more then high hp. Good gas milage wouldnt hurt either. Any Ideas?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Shaun41178||11-06-2000||01:09 AM||||Well if you are not going to be really racing it and are not really looking for high hp ones then I would just stick with the stock ones.  They provide good low end torque.  Porting them out would be a good thing to do to help get a bit more top end without breaking the bank.<P>I provide porting them as a service on an exchange basis. I have another thread in the Tech section titled about ported stock exhaust manifolds.  If interested then let me know.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||11-06-2000||08:21 AM||||Sean get a hold of Mike (Hytnotise) he's making a sweet set if your dead set on headers!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||11-06-2000||09:24 AM||||I have FOCOA in my 3.4. Took me 7 weeks to have them built last march. If you want more torque I agree with Shaun that the best bet is to keep the stock manifolds and just port them. If you still want headers then try to get dyno numbers before you buy anything if what you really want is more torque than HP. Otherwise you may be disappointed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||11-06-2000||06:05 PM||||No, I am not really dead set on headers, I am going to live with a set of ported stock manifolds for now. I am going to have Shaun port them for me. That will save alot of money I was thinking I would have to spend. I think they will do fine, for now. Thanks<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||11-06-2000||08:00 PM||||It has been my understanding that headers do not aversely affect your bottom-end torque like it did in 1fast2m6-dyno sheets. When you add headers to a car one of the things it does to the engine is change the fuel mixture to a lean condition. If you do not compensate for this than your power suffers. I know that the computer can compensate for fuel mixtures but that is only under a cruise condition. When you floor your car it goes into closed loop mode and the fuel mixture is than just calculated from a table stored in the prom chip, which is based on load, throttle position, and engine temperature. That table does not change when you change something on your engine. I have seen dyno sheets for other engines with headers compared to stock manifolds and they do loose some torque below 2000 rpm but more than make up for this above 2000 rpm when the fuel mixture is just right. <P>Don’t flame me for saying this, I am just making a point that I think may be true. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Terrybogin||11-06-2000||09:31 PM||||Headers will make an improvement only when the factory system is the bottle neck in performance. Because the 2.8's (and probably the 3.4's) are not heavy breathers, AND there is an actual DYNO sheet to back up the facts, I'd say large tube headers might not be the best dollar you could spend. But that is just my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||11-07-2000||05:01 PM||||What about small tube headers like the FOCOA headers? ||||reg||1
